{"gid":"2026_02_CLE_TB","week":2,"away":"CLE","home":"TB","away_col":"#FF7A45","home_col":"#FF5A4D","spread":6.5,"total":42.5,"fav":"TB","hpts":24,"apts":18,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 20 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 TB are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.43 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>The <strong>TB</strong> host the <strong>CLE</strong> as favorites of <strong>6.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>42.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>TB 24\u201318</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>40% of its snaps with TB in front</strong>, <strong>36% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with TB chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>TB should control</strong>. A favorite of 6 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>TB</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>CLE</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>CLE</strong> to run more total plays than TB \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for CLE's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong> \u2014 TB are stingy against the run (99 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Deshaun Watson</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 TB allow <strong>259 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> has room to work \u2014 TB allow <strong>40 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 TB give up 27 rec yds/game to backs, and CLE target the RB on 18% of throws.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 TB hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 TB are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.43 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CLE throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to TB \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Bucky Irving</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>TB \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>TB are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Bucky Irving</strong> (18 carries, 81 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though TB's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Emeka Egbuka</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 59 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Cade Otton</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 50 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>CLE \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>CLE are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 CLE project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 55 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, CLE funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>9 targets for 68 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 61 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CLE","col":"#FF7A45","players":[{"name":"Deshaun Watson","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":193.25,"book":175.5,"over_th":181.19,"under_th":201.72,"over_am":"\u2212110","under_am":"